% Factors and precedential constraint WITHOUT new keywords
% (a response to LE_extensions_proposal §3.2).
%
% Horty's result model — a precedent decided an open-textured issue on its
% pro-factors and con-factors; a later situation with at least those
% pro-factors and at most those con-factors cannot be decided the other way —
% written in plain Logical English plus two constructs that exist anyway:
% provenance trailers (the case base cites its decisions) and "otherwise"
% (the hook: decide by precedent when a precedent forces the issue, otherwise
% by a recorded judgment, which may still be open).
%
% - Factors are ordinary rules naming a factor: "a damage has factor
%   suddenness if the damage occurred suddenly."
% - The case base is ordinary facts about the cases, with their citations.
% - The constraint is two rules with "for all cases in which", in the
%   includable library precedent.le (the result model; the reason model would
%   be the same library with priority facts).
% - forced: the bathroom leak has the precedent's pro-factor and none of its
%   con-factors, so kitchen flood decides it: no judgment needed.
% - undecided: the garage leak has kitchen flood's pro-factor but also a
%   con-factor kitchen flood lacked (deliberate damage); and it has a
%   pro-factor garden shed lacked (suddenness). Neither precedent forces it: a
%   judgment is needed.
% - against: the broken window has only garden shed's con-factor, so garden
%   shed forces it the other way: not payable, whatever anyone judged.
% - The consistency of the case base is itself a query.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base precedent pattern includes these resources:
    precedent.

the templates are:
    *a claim* is payable.
    *a claim* is for *a damage*; undefined.
    *a damage* is reported within the time limit; undefined.
    *a damage* is accidental; judged.
    *a damage* counts as accidental.
    *a damage* occurred suddenly; undefined.
    *a damage* results from wear and tear; undefined.
    *a damage* was caused deliberately by the policyholder; undefined.

the knowledge base precedent pattern includes:

a claim is payable
    if the claim is for a damage
    and the damage is reported within the time limit
    and the damage counts as accidental.

% The hook: precedent first; otherwise the recorded (or open) judgment,
% unless a precedent forces the other way.
a damage counts as accidental
    if the damage is forced for accidental by a case
    otherwise the damage is accidental
    and it is not the case that
        the damage is forced against accidental by a case.

% Factors of the issue "accidental".
a damage has factor suddenness if the damage occurred suddenly.
a damage has factor wear and tear if the damage results from wear and tear.
a damage has factor deliberate damage if the damage was caused deliberately by the policyholder.

suddenness favours accidental.
wear and tear disfavours accidental.
deliberate damage disfavours accidental.

% The case base.
kitchen flood has factor suddenness, as stated in decision D-1 at paragraph 4.
kitchen flood has factor wear and tear, as stated in decision D-1 at paragraph 5.
kitchen flood decided for accidental,
    according to the ombudsman, as stated in decision D-1 at paragraph 9,
    because "suddenness outweighs the age of the pipe where no maintenance failure is shown".
garden shed has factor deliberate damage, as stated in decision D-2 at paragraph 3.
garden shed decided against accidental,
    according to the ombudsman, as stated in decision D-2 at paragraph 6,
    because "damage the policyholder caused on purpose is not an accident".

scenario forced is:
    claim two is for the bathroom leak, as stated in the claim form at section 2.
    the bathroom leak is reported within the time limit, as stated in the claim log at entry 8.
    the bathroom leak occurred suddenly, as stated in the plumber report at page 1.
    payable expects answers ["claim two is payable"].
    consistency expects answers [].

scenario undecided is:
    claim three is for the garage leak, as stated in the claim form at section 2.
    the garage leak is reported within the time limit, as stated in the claim log at entry 9.
    the garage leak occurred suddenly, as stated in the plumber report at page 1.
    the garage leak was caused deliberately by the policyholder, according to the insurer.
    payable expects answers ["claim three is payable"] and unknowns ["the garage leak is accidental"].

scenario against is:
    claim four is for the broken window, as stated in the claim form at section 2.
    the broken window is reported within the time limit, as stated in the claim log at entry 10.
    the broken window was caused deliberately by the policyholder, according to the insurer.
    the broken window is accidental, according to the policyholder.
    payable expects answers [].

query payable is:
    which claim is payable.

query consistency is:
    which issue has an inconsistent case base.
